WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.680
When a long-running TV series comes to an
end, fans can sometimes get a little bit emotional.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.220
But plenty of Game of Thrones enthusiasts
aren't just sad about the show ending - they're

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.860
mostly feeling angry about how it all wrapped
up.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.860
That unrest has given rise to a petition demanding
HBO remake the eighth and final season with

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.940
a whole new team of writers, a tall order
that they hope at least sends a clear message

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.730
to the people in charge of the show.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.160
"There's a fierceness you don't often see.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.780
They'd do anything to protect their babies."

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:32.020
The online petition gained international attention
as the final episode of the series drew near.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:37.180
By the day after the divisive finale, it had
already received well over a million signatures.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.040
One person who was unaware of the petition's
existence was the actress whose unexpected

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.700
character turn arguably started the intense
fan outcry in the first place.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.000
Daenerys Targaryen actress Emilia Clarke was
told about the petition when she sat down

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.609
for an interview with the New Yorker, during
which she gamely offered her ideas for a hypothetical

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:54.280
"do-over."

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.499
But Clarke also said, she's, quote, "in no
position to critique the geniuses that have

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.600
written eight seasons’ worth of wonderful
stuff."

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.980
"Not a true story, necessarily."

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.420
Clarke revealed that she would have loved
if Daenerys interacted with her handmaiden,

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.080
Missandei, and her greatest enemy, Cersei
Lannister, more than she did during Season

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:11.580
8.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.780
She also said she wanted to, quote, "see a
bit more" discussion between characters during

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.340
the controversial fifth episode of the season,
which followed Daenerys and her allied forces

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.660
as they stormed King's Landing.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.659
When the city surrendered in a peaceful manner
during the climactic episode, the Mother of

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:30.259
Dragons suddenly made a heel turn toward sheer
madness, ordering her dragon to burn down

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:31.320
everything in sight.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:35.040
Clarke has since said that Dany's tumbling
into madness was inevitable, but she would

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.829
have enjoyed additional conversations being
included in between all the action.

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:40.100
She explained to the New Yorker,

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:41.540
"The genocide was there.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:42.800
That was always going to happen.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.780
And I just think [...] that we are more than
happy to contently sit there and watch ten

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:48.159
minutes of two people talking, because it's
beautiful.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.800
I just wanted to see a bit more of that."

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.360
Clarke also expressed a desire, in her idea
of a hypothetical do-over, to see Dany's,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.619
quote, "last images being that of kind of
a hopeful, childlike love" before she dies

00:01:58.619 --> 00:02:03.450
in the arms of her lover-slash-nephew, Jon
Snow, who tragically slays her out of a certainty

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.280
that she'll cause more harm than good to the
people of Westeros.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.619
Clarke said Daenerys' death was a, quote,
"shocker to read," but the actress also admitted

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:13.459
that she isn't sure the character could have
had a more triumphant ending.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:14.100
As she put it,

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.040
"I always knew that the show was never going
to satisfy everyone.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.879
[...] The stories are too vast, the characters
too complex.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.860
The show is, in a certain way, divisive: 'Whose
side are you on?'

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.020
Also, if you're pleasing everyone, then it's
probably quite tepid.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.060
But to me it seemed like the only way it could
end."

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.810
It couldn't have been easy for Clarke to come
to terms with what happened to her character,

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.950
whom she has played since 2011, on the final
two episodes of Game of Thrones.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.900
Her response to the petition asking for a
re-do is incredibly level-headed - which is

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.120
admirable considering Clarke's Daenerys was
the only major character to die in the final

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:44.620
episode.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:48.300
It's unlikely that those who hated Game of
Thrones' final season will suddenly change

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.530
their minds after hearing Clarke's response
to the online backlash, but perhaps her diplomatic

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.040
disposition might inspire them to ease up
a little.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.740
If the woman who had to play the character
who went mad, killed thousands, and got stabbed

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:02.900
to death over the course of two episodes can
remain calm about the situation, the people

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.900
who only had to witness the calamity should
be able to do the same.

